WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.255
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:08.680
AGV: Offering services
every day to thousands

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:11.560
and even millions of people
is an amazing challenge

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:12.880
from a scale perspective.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.149
More specifically, take
workforce development programs

00:00:16.149 --> 00:00:17.770
run by cities and states.

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:19.640
They are extremely important.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.550
And as they support
so many people

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:23.170
with an array of
services that range

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:27.220
from job-matching opportunities,
personal development via job

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:31.220
coaching, self-service
resources, to just name a few.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:33.430
And I have learned
a lot about how

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:37.150
running such meaningful programs
requires extensive coordination

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:41.440
across applicants, employers,
community partners, and sharing

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.660
knowledge broadly.

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:46.540
2020 brought many
challenges, especially

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:49.390
as in-person operations
were shut down

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:51.940
and many were left
vulnerable to unemployment.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:54.460
The State of Rhode Island
responded to these challenges

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:57.430
by modernizing their workforce
development operations

00:00:57.430 --> 00:01:00.160
and moving completely
to an online platform

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.020
called the Virtual
Career Center--

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.150
also nicknamed VCC.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:06.550
It was developed in
partnership with Google Cloud

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:09.730
and a nonprofit called Research
Improving People's Lives.

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:13.660
And the VCC was fully built
by Google Cloud partner Maven

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:14.380
Wave.

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:17.770
As a quick intro, Maven
Wave helps a wide array

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:20.590
of organizations, including
public sector customers,

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:22.910
with many types of
cloud initiatives--

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:24.670
whether it's big
data and machine

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:27.250
learning, building
mobile applications,

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:30.760
helping design positive
online user experiences,

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:32.890
moving infrastructure
to the cloud,

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:37.000
or deploying collaboration tools
like email, chat, documents.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.880
They even offer strategy
and technology advice.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:42.610
Today, I've invited Joel
Osman from Maven Wave

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:44.330
to share more
about that journey.

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:45.220
Howdy, my friend.

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:47.560
Mind sharing a bit
about what you do?

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:50.290
JOEL OSMAN: So I lead the
Maven Wave digital experience

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:50.830
design--

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:53.500
our UX and design team-- as
well as our custom application

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:54.730
development practice.

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:58.270
And I am also the executive
sponsor for our Virtual Career

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:01.240
Center solution that we've been
bringing to market with Google.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:02.710
AGV: Joel, for the
folks watching,

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:05.110
could you briefly share,
what was the specific use

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:06.741
case your team focused on?

00:02:06.741 --> 00:02:08.949
JOEL OSMAN: So the primary
use case that we looked at

00:02:08.949 --> 00:02:11.920
is, how can we apply
leading-edge, emerging

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.560
technologies to help
people get back to work?

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.320
And to use those
tools in such a way

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:21.460
that we can augment the
personal one-to-one interactions

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:24.280
that these agencies had
been using with job seekers

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:25.660
to help them get back to work.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:26.875
AGV: That's beautiful.

00:02:26.875 --> 00:02:28.990
Joel, can you touch upon
some of the products

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:31.270
it took to build the
Virtual Career Center?

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:34.180
JOEL OSMAN: The VCC comprises
a number of different Google

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:35.320
technologies.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:37.180
Obviously, the custom
pieces that we're

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:41.200
building for scheduling,
for login authentication,

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:42.910
for data pipelines
and data analytics

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:44.320
are all in Google
Cloud platform.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:46.000
And we're doing
custom builds there

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.862
that integrate into the
state's existing systems.

00:02:48.862 --> 00:02:50.320
So we essentially
have taken things

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:53.800
like Google Meet, Google
Calendar, Google Docs--

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:56.080
so a lot of those core
tool sets that people

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:57.790
use in their
everyday lives, we've

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:00.580
wrapped them in this specific
use case around the Virtual

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:01.390
Career Center.

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:04.870
So when a job coach
is going online

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:08.470
to update their availability
of when they can hold sessions,

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:11.128
that's really happening, in the
background, in Google Calendar.

00:03:11.128 --> 00:03:12.670
And when a job seeker
and a job coach

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:14.733
get together in a
video session, it's

00:03:14.733 --> 00:03:16.150
actually happening
in Google Meet.

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:19.970
So those are really the engines
of technologies, if you will,

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:22.083
that are driving the
entire VCC experience.

00:03:22.083 --> 00:03:23.500
AGV: Now, if you
don't mind, let's

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:25.360
do a quick walkthrough
of what I've learned

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:26.710
about your architecture.

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:28.990
And then I would like to
talk a bit more about some

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:32.170
of the design decisions
behind all of this.

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:34.570
For starters, end
users mainly interact

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:37.300
via an app built on
Angular as the frontend,

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.400
and it's connected to
backend services hosted

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.970
on Google Kubernetes Engine
or relevant Google Workspace

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:45.310
products, such as
Chat, Calendar, Meet,

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:46.720
Drive, and Docs.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:49.630
From a user journey perspective,
there are three main types

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:52.120
of users at this time,
and that's job coaches,

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:54.400
job seekers, and employers.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.510
Job coaches all have Google IDs
in the Google Workspace domain

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:01.510
and therefore authenticate
against the Google Identity

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:02.530
repository.

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:05.740
Job seekers are authenticated
through a Cognito-based process

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:08.110
maintained by the nonprofit
I mentioned earlier

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:09.850
with the Rhode
Island infrastructure

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:12.670
team and the Department
of information Technology.

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:15.730
For context, prior to the
start of the VCC project,

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:18.790
the state of Rhode Island was
making use of this Cognito

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:21.760
identity repository
to authenticate users

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.790
going through the unemployment
insurance application process.

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:28.180
And rather than adding an
additional identity repository

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:30.040
for users interacting
with the state,

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:31.840
the decision was
made to leverage

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:34.630
the existing Cognito
repository and its methods

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:36.160
of authentication.

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:38.470
In this release of the
Virtual Career Center,

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:40.990
employers participate
directly with Google Meet--

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:43.030
and to an extent,
Google Calendar--

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:45.340
but not the Angular app.

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:48.490
To touch upon some of the Google
Cloud components specifically,

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:50.140
let's start with
Firestore, which

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:52.930
is a real-time database
that keeps data in sync

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:54.460
across client apps.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.530
It is used as the
primary persistence

00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:58.070
tool for the web pages.

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:00.730
It also has a small role
to play with the login

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:02.800
process for job coaches.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:06.080
Next is BigQuery, which is a
serverless warehouse for data,

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:08.110
which is why it
is used primarily

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:10.000
for reporting purposes.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.900
Data Studio is used to build
filterable dashboards over it.

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:15.850
Then there are Cloud
Functions, which

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:18.460
are functions you can
trigger via snippets of code

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:21.430
and are used for a few
purposes in the solution,

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:25.120
such as populating jobs and
training data in the production

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:27.940
instance, ensuring
consistency between Google

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:30.880
Calendar and availability
data for job coaches,

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:34.150
and for populating BigQuery
on a scheduled basis.

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:35.770
Then there's the
Kubernetes cluster

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:38.590
on which server-side code
runs in a single region

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:41.830
deployment with a minimum
of four nodes per zone

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:45.580
across three zones of
the US East Region.

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:47.935
The cluster is set
to autoscale in order

00:05:47.935 --> 00:05:49.810
to ensure that there is
sufficient processing

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:51.190
power in the event
that there are

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:54.250
spikes in activity on the site.

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:55.840
From a security
standpoint, there's

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:58.390
Cloud Armor, which protects
applications and websites

00:05:58.390 --> 00:05:59.230
from attacks.

00:05:59.230 --> 00:06:01.210
And in this specific
implementation,

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:04.390
there are several sets of
NIST-compliant policies--

00:06:04.390 --> 00:06:07.270
such as geofencing--
which prohibits access

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:10.870
to the site from
outside of the US.

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:13.930
Content is accessed through
Google's content distribution

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:17.690
network, taking advantage of
caching wherever possible.

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:19.780
And finally, to
manage the lifecycle

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:22.180
of the infrastructure,
a Terraform script

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:24.790
is used, which is
an open-source tool

00:06:24.790 --> 00:06:28.240
and is structured into
five folder environments.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:30.310
Can you share a bit
more about, what

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:33.310
was the experience
like for users before

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:35.962
and after the Virtual
Career Center was launched?

00:06:35.962 --> 00:06:36.670
JOEL OSMAN: Sure.

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:39.130
So in a sense, the
solution that we built,

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:41.800
it changes the way
job seekers interact

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:44.080
with the state and
the state agency.

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:46.240
Before the Virtual
Career Center,

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:48.132
interested people--
there was a website

00:06:48.132 --> 00:06:50.590
and there were website resources
that people would utilize.

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:52.960
But it was very
much, in many states,

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:55.150
a hodgepodge of things--

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:56.710
the training opportunities.

00:06:56.710 --> 00:06:59.290
There was often a
state database of jobs

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:00.460
that they could search into.

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:03.320
And there were some very
basic online capabilities.

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:05.080
But a lot of things
happened physically

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:06.460
in the Career Center.

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:09.310
There's a lot of that
face-to-face interaction

00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:13.750
where coaches could really have
an impact and help job seekers.

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:16.270
And the challenge, when we
built the Virtual Career Center,

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:18.160
is how can we keep
that intimacy?

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:20.650
How can we keep that
collaborative nature?

00:07:20.650 --> 00:07:23.297
And how can we dial
up the online tools,

00:07:23.297 --> 00:07:24.880
in a sense-- because
in many respects,

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:28.750
we were forced to-- but try to
keep that tight interaction?

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:31.990
We strive to keep that
personal interaction.

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:35.050
And I like to say now, a job
coach can talk to a job seeker

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:37.330
on opposite ends of the
state and they can really

00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:39.700
focus on getting
the right advice,

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:42.250
the right coach in front
of the right job seeker.

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:44.140
AGV: It's exciting
to me to see how

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:47.770
we can continue to
recreate these safe spaces

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:49.930
in a virtual format,
and on top of that,

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:53.710
create better alignment between
job coaches and job seekers

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:57.020
who have expertise in
navigating different services.

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:00.310
This is thanks to having more
robust ways for job seekers

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:03.010
to find coaches with
specific backgrounds,

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:05.750
such as coaches who have a
history with helping college

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:06.250
students.

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:09.190
And Joel, is there a
fun fact you could share

00:08:09.190 --> 00:08:11.050
with us about the project?

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:13.660
JOEL OSMAN: Well, I can
share one interesting fact.

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:16.750
Before we launched the solution
with Rhode Island, which

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:21.190
was the initial Virtual Career
Center state that we deployed

00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:24.220
in, we were a little
concerned about the adoption

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:25.480
from the career coaches.

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:28.960
They were already having to
deal with working from home.

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:30.970
We showed them what it
was going to be like.

00:08:30.970 --> 00:08:33.789
We tried to do everything we
could to ease them into it.

00:08:33.789 --> 00:08:36.400
And we did it with sort
of holding our breath.

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:38.500
What's going to come of this?

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:40.090
We were actually
pleasantly surprised.

00:08:40.090 --> 00:08:43.690
They were absolutely
excited for the solution.

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:46.210
They were commenting how it's
really going to help them out.

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:47.002
They couldn't wait.

00:08:47.002 --> 00:08:48.800
They were asking how
soon they can get it.

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:50.050
So it was kind of interesting.

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:51.340
I think we were overly--

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:52.460
not pessimistic.

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:55.600
We were more concerned of
what the reactions would be.

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:58.090
But they just embraced
it wholeheartedly

00:08:58.090 --> 00:08:59.230
and were very excited.

00:08:59.230 --> 00:09:01.835
And it got us very excited that
we are doing the right thing

00:09:01.835 --> 00:09:03.460
and we've created
the right experience.

00:09:03.460 --> 00:09:06.730
And just seeing them absolutely
pounce on the solution

00:09:06.730 --> 00:09:07.910
was very gratifying.

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:08.410
AGV: Yeah.

00:09:08.410 --> 00:09:11.260
I think that fear of
whether a new tool is going

00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:13.570
to be well-received
or not, especially

00:09:13.570 --> 00:09:15.970
amongst so much change
in the environment,

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:19.280
is extremely relatable
to IT practitioners.

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:21.460
And so your approach
is an excellent example

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:25.060
of how important it is to scope
time to collect user feedback

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:28.720
and to do it very early on
and as often as possible

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:30.290
when designing systems.

00:09:30.290 --> 00:09:32.680
Another topic I want
to touch upon is data.

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:36.430
By having more data centralized
in a digital format,

00:09:36.430 --> 00:09:39.250
there are greater opportunities
around analytics and insights.

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:42.055
What were some design
considerations with data?

00:09:42.055 --> 00:09:44.680
JOEL OSMAN: We're working a lot
with data in the Virtual Career

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:45.760
Center solution.

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:47.650
In particular, we're
looking at analytics.

00:09:47.650 --> 00:09:50.050
If we're going to implement
a solution like this,

00:09:50.050 --> 00:09:51.940
key stakeholders in
the state, right up

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:53.230
to the governor's offices--

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:55.090
they want to know,
is it working?

00:09:55.090 --> 00:09:56.860
Are people finding jobs?

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:58.480
Are people having
career sessions?

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:01.268
So there's a lot of
analytics to track

00:10:01.268 --> 00:10:02.560
what's working and not working.

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:04.780
And we can do this in
a virtualized solution.

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:07.780
And we talk a lot about creating
analytics and dashboards,

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:09.935
not just for stakeholders
within the state who

00:10:09.935 --> 00:10:12.310
are interested in how the
Virtual Career Center is doing,

00:10:12.310 --> 00:10:14.230
but we talk a lot
about employers.

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:16.450
Because a lot of times,
employers are posting jobs

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:18.490
and want to use those
Virtual Career Centers

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:19.847
to connect to job seekers.

00:10:19.847 --> 00:10:21.680
So we're talking about
an employer dashboard

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:23.920
so they can understand,
who's out there?

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:26.020
Who's the possible
candidate pool?

00:10:26.020 --> 00:10:29.410
And how can they see
how the Virtual Career

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:30.580
Center can benefit them?

00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:32.770
And we even talk about
personal dashboards.

00:10:32.770 --> 00:10:35.920
AGV: Do you envision any
technical enhancements

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:38.350
in the future to
this architecture?

00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:41.550
And is this solution only
for state and local entities?

00:10:41.550 --> 00:10:43.300
JOEL OSMAN: So the
Virtual Career Center--

00:10:43.300 --> 00:10:45.160
or the VCC, as we
call it-- is actually

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:48.160
comprised of three pillars,
is the way we talk about it.

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:50.650
One is around modernizing
digital experiences.

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:55.240
And this is taking what is, for
many states, an older design,

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:56.770
older set of
technologies that they

00:10:56.770 --> 00:10:59.290
used for very basic
online functionality,

00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:02.440
and giving that all a new,
modern look, a modern face,

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:05.620
a central place for people
to go for all their needs

00:11:05.620 --> 00:11:08.420
and requirements as a job
seeker within the state.

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:09.730
So that's the modernizing.

00:11:09.730 --> 00:11:11.860
The second pillar is
around new innovations.

00:11:11.860 --> 00:11:14.350
Can we bring machine learning
and artificial intelligence

00:11:14.350 --> 00:11:15.520
to job searches?

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:17.740
Can we help with
resume-building or resume

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:20.710
parsing, where we can actually
take keywords out of a resume

00:11:20.710 --> 00:11:24.640
and match those to certain
jobs, more of in a clustering

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:28.310
machine learning model rather
than just a raw keyword search?

00:11:28.310 --> 00:11:30.145
We also looked at chat
bots and the ability

00:11:30.145 --> 00:11:32.020
to take some of the load
off the call centers

00:11:32.020 --> 00:11:35.320
in states and utilizing
natural language processing

00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:38.020
to help guide job seekers
and answer questions.

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:40.870
So and we looked at a number
of those types of innovations.

00:11:40.870 --> 00:11:42.850
The third pillar in the
Virtual Career Center

00:11:42.850 --> 00:11:45.790
is around taking what was a
brick-and-mortar, physical

00:11:45.790 --> 00:11:47.800
career center and
putting that online

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:49.480
and doing it
virtually using Google

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:51.190
Meet and other
technologies to create

00:11:51.190 --> 00:11:53.325
a virtual version of
what was happening

00:11:53.325 --> 00:11:54.700
in those physical
career centers.

00:11:54.700 --> 00:11:56.320
So far, every state
we've talked to, they

00:11:56.320 --> 00:11:57.670
tend to choose different things.

00:11:57.670 --> 00:12:00.170
It's interesting, because you
think they all need the same--

00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:01.940
they have the same needs.

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:06.430
But their own history,
their technology savviness,

00:12:06.430 --> 00:12:08.050
the needs of their
constituents--

00:12:08.050 --> 00:12:08.860
it tends to vary.

00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:11.420
And so we find different
commissioner stakeholders,

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:12.670
different states--

00:12:12.670 --> 00:12:14.120
they need different pieces.

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:15.490
And that's why it's nice
to be able to put out

00:12:15.490 --> 00:12:16.960
a-- we always call it the menu.

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:19.180
We tell states, you
can order off the menu.

00:12:19.180 --> 00:12:21.130
And depending what you
order, obviously, they

00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:22.510
have prices, just like a menu.

00:12:22.510 --> 00:12:24.940
And so that's how we work
through budgets and solutions

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:25.300
with them.

00:12:25.300 --> 00:12:27.010
And it's been interesting
to see different states.

00:12:27.010 --> 00:12:28.450
So beyond just
state governments,

00:12:28.450 --> 00:12:30.492
we've actually had some
interesting opportunities

00:12:30.492 --> 00:12:33.010
to talk about the Virtual Career
Center and the technologies

00:12:33.010 --> 00:12:35.380
and the solution
with some counties,

00:12:35.380 --> 00:12:40.270
with some other countries,
and a number of universities.

00:12:40.270 --> 00:12:42.820
Everywhere you've got
people looking for jobs,

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:44.680
or people looking for
additional training,

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:47.133
or looking to better
their careers,

00:12:47.133 --> 00:12:49.300
there's an opportunity in
the Virtual Career Center.

00:12:49.300 --> 00:12:51.490
AGV: Well, thank you,
Joel, for walking us

00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:54.490
through so much great
information about your journey

00:12:54.490 --> 00:12:55.510
today.

00:12:55.510 --> 00:12:58.060
To wrap up and leave
on a positive note,

00:12:58.060 --> 00:13:00.853
what was a memorable
moment you experienced?

00:13:00.853 --> 00:13:02.770
JOEL OSMAN: One of the
things that I have said

00:13:02.770 --> 00:13:05.170
is, throughout the pandemic--

00:13:05.170 --> 00:13:09.510
I think for me personally, and
I think for a lot of the team--

00:13:09.510 --> 00:13:11.120
we're not frontline workers.

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:12.370
We're not health care workers.

00:13:12.370 --> 00:13:13.870
We're not grocery store workers.

00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:17.620
And we were along for the
ride, in some respects,

00:13:17.620 --> 00:13:18.620
during this whole thing.

00:13:18.620 --> 00:13:20.500
And so being able to
be part of a solution

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:22.570
that is doing our part--

00:13:22.570 --> 00:13:24.220
we may be on the IT side of it.

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:29.410
But just being able to
do something in our field

00:13:29.410 --> 00:13:31.690
to help people's
lives get better,

00:13:31.690 --> 00:13:33.872
to help people find jobs
coming out of the pandemic,

00:13:33.872 --> 00:13:35.830
get a job that maybe they
lost to the pandemic,

00:13:35.830 --> 00:13:37.720
it really felt
like doing our part

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:40.730
versus being on the sidelines
through this whole crisis.

00:13:40.730 --> 00:13:41.230
AGV: Ah yes.

00:13:41.230 --> 00:13:43.280
This work is quite meaningful.

00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:45.010
And I think it's
an epic milestone

00:13:45.010 --> 00:13:48.010
to be able to scale
operations to a virtual format

00:13:48.010 --> 00:13:51.760
in this way, which speaks to the
beauty of building in the cloud

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:55.250
and designing for a
positive user experience.

00:13:55.250 --> 00:13:57.340
Well friends, I left
several helpful links

00:13:57.340 --> 00:13:58.900
in this video's
description in case

00:13:58.900 --> 00:14:00.190
you want to check them out.

